      "caption": "Three peaks, the middle one highest, sitting on a rough line near 100. Traders call this a head and shoulders. Nothing here is a forecast — it is a description of fifteen days that already happened."
    },
    {
      "id": "c4",
      "type": "binary_choice",
      "prompt": "You spot a textbook head and shoulders in a chart of last year's prices. What does that tell you about tomorrow?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "repeats",
          "label": "The shape reliably repeats",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "id": "nothing",
          "label": "Nothing, on its own",
          "correct": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The shape was labelled **after** the prices printed. Naming it costs nothing and proves nothing. A pattern only means something if you know how often it failed, and one chart cannot tell you that."
    },
    {
      "id": "c5",
      "type": "concept",
      "headline": "Random data grows shapes too.",
      "body": "Plot a few hundred coin flips as a running total and you will get trends, pullbacks, and clean triangles. No company, no news, no buyers — just noise. If a shape can appear with no cause behind it, seeing one proves nothing.",
      "art": {
        "kind": "glyph",
        "name": "brain",
        "bg": "lilac"
      }
    },
