    {
      "id": "c7",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "body": "Slide the starting point a few days and a flag becomes lower highs. The label depends on where you begin the [[1]].",
      "wordBank": [
        "count",
        "trend",
        "session"
      ],
      "answer": "count",
      "explanation": "A pattern is not a property of the price. It is a property of the window you drew around the price."
    },
    {
      "id": "c8",
      "type": "concept",
      "headline": "Textbook examples are picked after the ending.",
      "body": "Every pattern in a book was chosen because it worked. The author scrolled through years of charts and kept the ones that did the expected thing. The failures were never photographed. You are looking at a highlight reel, not a sample.",
      "art": {
        "kind": "glyph",
        "name": "star",
        "bg": "blush"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c9",
      "type": "sticker_reveal",
      "body": "Before you accept any pattern claim, three questions. None of them are about the shape itself.",
      "stickers": [
        {
          "label": "How often did it fail?",
          "art": {
            "kind": "glyph",
            "name": "warning",
            "bg": "butter"
          },
          "anchor": {
            "x": 0.32,
            "y": 0.18
          },
          "rotation": -5
        },
        {
          "label": "Who counted, over what?",
          "art": {
            "kind": "glyph",
            "name": "magnifier",
            "bg": "sky"
          },
          "anchor": {
            "x": 0.68,
            "y": 0.5
          },
          "rotation": 6
        },
        {
          "label": "What would disprove it?",
          "art": {
            "kind": "glyph",
            "name": "scale",
            "bg": "mint"
          },
          "anchor": {
            "x": 0.36,
            "y": 0.82
          },
          "rotation": -3
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c10",
      "type": "concept",
      "headline": "Ask for the denominator.",
      "body": "A pattern claim is a batting average with the denominator missing. Ten charts where it worked tell you nothing until you know how many times the same shape appeared and did the opposite. That number is the whole claim.",
      "art": {
        "kind": "glyph",
        "name": "percent",
        "bg": "forest"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c11",
      "type": "main_idea",
      "title": "What patterns are good for",
      "summary": "Patterns are a vocabulary for describing what a chart already did, not a machine for predicting what it will do.",
      "bullets": [
        "Head and shoulders, triangles, and flags are named shapes — named after the fact.",
        "Random data produces the same shapes, so seeing one is not evidence.",
        "The same prices carry different labels depending on where you start the count.",
        "Textbook examples are selected on their outcomes. The failures are never shown."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c12",
      "type": "key_insight",
      "art": {
        "kind": "glyph",
        "name": "eye",
        "bg": "ink"
      },
      "insight": "A chart is a record, not a forecast. Every pattern on it was named after the ending was already known.",
